WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.930
Build a solid from little unit cubes.

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:05.930
From one angle you can see the front and the top,

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:07.510
but some cubes are hidden.

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:10.540
The layers give you a way to count every cube

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:11.270
in the solid.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:13.690
Think of lifting one level at a time.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:18.360
In AMC Upper Primary practice, a picture might ask how

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:19.690
many cubes make a shape.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:23.520
In a Rossmoyne style spatial puzzle, the diagram may

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:24.990
show holes or an opening.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.500
Sketch a plan of each layer so you can track

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:28.590
which cubes stay.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.890
This block is three cubes wide, two deep, and two high.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:35.170
How many unit cubes does it contain?

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:38.140
Think about how many positions there are on just

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:38.990
the bottom layer.

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:45.910
One layer has two rows, with three cubes in each row.

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:47.460
That gives six cubes.

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.380
The upper layer has exactly the same six positions.

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:53.970
Together there are twelve cubes, including cubes the

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:55.360
outside drawing hides.

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:57.740
A block has six stacks.

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:02.100
Their heights, front row first, are one, three, two,

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:04.040
then four, one, three.

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:05.980
All stacks rest on the table.

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:07.980
How many cubes are in the block?

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:10.900
Try counting level by level from the table up.

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:16.270
Level one has six cubes.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:18.770
Four of those stacks reach level two.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:20.870
Three stacks reach level three,

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:22.650
and one reaches level four.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.890
Add six, four, three and one to get fourteen.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:29.030
Summing the six heights gives fourteen as well.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:30.850
That is an independent check.

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:34.550
Start with a solid four by four by three block.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:37.130
Remove a vertical column of three cubes.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:40.740
Also remove a straight tunnel of four cubes across

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:41.630
the middle layer.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.970
The tunnel passes through the same column.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:45.490
How many cubes remain?

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:51.130
Each of the three layers originally

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:52.760
contained sixteen cubes.

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:56.460
Removing the vertical column takes one from every layer.

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.010
The tunnel runs through four positions on

00:01:59.010 --> 00:01:59.820
the middle layer.

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:01.980
One of them is already empty.

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:04.440
The tunnel takes only three more cubes.

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:06.320
Count what's left.

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:09.120
The bottom and top each have fifteen cubes.

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:12.110
The middle has twelve because four of its sixteen

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:13.340
positions are empty.

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:16.400
Fifteen plus twelve plus fifteen is forty-two.

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:20.400
As a check, forty-eight minus six is also forty-two.

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:24.240
A visible cube count misses the cubes inside.

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:27.490
A second mistake is subtracting three and four when

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:28.340
their paths meet.

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:30.760
The shared cube can be removed once.

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:34.270
Mark the empty cells by layer or subtract the shared

00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:35.820
cell from the removal count.

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:39.590
In an exam, identify the base dimensions and the

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:40.520
number of levels.

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:43.300
Label hidden positions on a quick layer sketch.

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:46.280
When two cuts cross, mark their common cell.

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:48.880
Finally, check your count a second way,

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:51.460
using full volume minus the missing cubes.
